                      (    forth6809.asm):12054                                   ; frame correctly). The one combination that
                      (    forth6809.asm):12055                                   ; broke was UNLOOP immediately before EXIT -
                      (    forth6809.asm):12056                                   ; UNLOOP discarding 6 of the 8 bytes itself,
                      (    forth6809.asm):12057                                   ; then EXITUNLOOP unconditionally discarding
                      (    forth6809.asm):12058                                   ; a full 8 more, overshooting into whatever
                      (    forth6809.asm):12059                                   ; sat below (typically the caller's own
                      (    forth6809.asm):12060                                   ; return address) and branching into random
                      (    forth6809.asm):12061                                   ; memory on return. Since UNLOOP has no
                      (    forth6809.asm):12062                                   ; other legitimate use than immediately
                      (    forth6809.asm):12063                                   ; preceding an exit from the definition, and
                      (    forth6809.asm):12064                                   ; EXIT already handles that correctly on its
                      (    forth6809.asm):12065                                   ; own, UNLOOP is now a true no-op rather than
